# rectangle_cutting:
# CSES 1744, Rectangle Cutting (https://cses.fi/problemset/task/1744), in our
# own words: a rectangle of whole units, first by second; a cut splits one
# rectangle in two along a line between units, from edge to edge; the fewest cuts
# after which every piece is a square. The input is two binary naturals
# (corpora/library/binary/natural), each at least 1, and the answer a binary
# natural. Part of the CSES port's third batch.
#
# THE PROGRAM, written by the definition: a square needs no cut, and any other
# rectangle is cut for the first time along some line, across its first side or
# across its second, at some distance from the edge, and the cuts of the whole
# are that cut plus the fewest cuts of each of the two pieces; the fewest cuts of
# the rectangle are the least over every such first cut. Under tabling the
# reading shares the rectangles it meets, about first times second rectangles
# each trying first plus second first cuts; the replacement the family asks for
# is the dynamic program over the table of rectangles, the same recurrence read
# from small rectangles to large, on fixed-width integers.
#
# DEFINITION 14: the program P, the point's term, is built of λ and application
# only: its text holds no top, no choice, no parallel and no literal. It β-reduces
# to V, the canonical binary numeral of the answer: every recursion ends, since
# the rectangle's area falls at every call (a first cut at distance cut, 1 <= cut
# < side, makes pieces of the sides cut and side - cut, both smaller than side), a square stops
# the recursion, and every other recursion consumes a finite list or a binary
# numeral; the branches that a Scott boolean discards are never read.
# binary_subtract, which writes no top (../common/binary_modular), is asked only
# for side - cut with cut < side. The least is taken of a nonempty list: the
# rectangle is no square, so one of its sides is at least 2 and has a first cut.
# So ⊢_β P = V, and P ≃_O V by the soundness of the derivable
# equations for the contextual preorder of Definition 14 (Lean
# `ContextualPreorder.contextualEquivalenceOfDerivableLatticeEta`): V is an
# answer, P ⊑_O V. No other closed βη normal form W has P ⊑_O W: then V ⊑_O W,
# while Böhm's theorem gives a pure context that takes V to λx y. x and W to
# λx y. y, and applying both to λa. a and Ω makes the first solvable and the
# second not.
λ export
. (λtuple. tuple λmixin. mixin mixin)
λ omega
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "prelude") "fixpoint")
λ fixpoint
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "prelude") "boolean")
λ _ false _ _
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "prelude") "list")
λ nil cons append _ _
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "binary") "natural")
λ binary_zero _ binary_odd _ _
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "binary") "arithmetic")
λ merge_binary_increment merge_binary_plus _ merge_binary_equality
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "binary") "order")
λ binary_less_than _ binary_minimum
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "problems") "cses") "common") "binary_modular")
λ _ binary_subtract _ _
. (λtuple. tuple (binary_odd binary_zero))
λ one
. ( λ tuple
    . tuple λ. fixpoint λself least candidates. candidates least λcandidate rest. self (binary_minimum least candidate) rest
  )
λ fold_minimum
. ( λ tuple
    . tuple
    λ cuts total other
    . fixpoint
      ( λ walk cut
        . binary_less_than cut total
          (cons (merge_binary_increment (merge_binary_plus (cuts cut other) (cuts (binary_subtract total cut false) other))) λ. walk (merge_binary_increment cut))
          nil
      ) one
  )
λ splits
. ( λ tuple
    . tuple
    λ. fixpoint
    λ self first_side second_side
    . merge_binary_equality first_side second_side binary_zero
      ( (append (splits self first_side second_side) λ. splits self second_side first_side)
        binary_zero λhead tail. fold_minimum head tail
      )
  )
λ rectangle_cuts
. (λtuple. tuple λfirst_side second_side. rectangle_cuts first_side second_side)
λ rectangle_cutting
. export rectangle_cutting
